Build the pthread kext into the kernel instead of loading it.

xnu has no pthread implementation of its own. bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c is a
table of function pointers and nothing more, and the only thing that ever fills
it is com.apple.kec.pthread -- the kext half of Apple's libpthread. A kernel
built from this tree alone therefore cannot create a user thread at all:
libpthread's runtime calls bsdthread_register() during the startup of every
process, and that call goes straight through the table.

There are no kexts in this port, so the kext is compiled into the kernel.

Three things make this smaller than it sounds. xnu-4903 already carries the
workqueue engine itself -- bsd/conf/files lists pthread_workqueue.c and
pthread_priority.c as standard -- so what remains in the kext is the psynch
locks, bsdthread_*, and registration: three sources, about 4200 lines.
Compatibility is structural rather than negotiated, because the kext includes
<sys/pthread_shims.h> from xnu, so the table is defined in exactly one place
and the static_asserts at the top of pthread_shims.c check both halves against
it. And the start routine is three lines:

    kern_return_t pthread_start(kmod_info_t *ki, void *d)
    {
            pthread_kext_register((pthread_functions_t)&pthread_internal_functions,
                &pthread_kern);
            return KERN_SUCCESS;
    }

Nothing calls it when the code is linked in, so pthread_init() calls it by hand
the first time the table is needed. The call is guarded on the table still
being NULL, so it happens once; pthread_kext_register() panics on a second
registration.

The sources are staged into bsd/pthread/kext by the build script, which fetches
libpthread-330.250.2 -- the 10.14.6 release train, the same one xnu-4903.270.47
comes from. They keep libpthread's own directory layout, because they include
each other by project-relative path ("kern/synch_internal.h", "pthread/qos.h",
"private/qos_private.h"), which is why the three objects get an -I pointing at
the staging root. The angle-bracket includes -- <pthread/bsdthread_private.h>,
<pthread/priority_private.h>, <pthread/workqueue_syscalls.h> -- are xnu's own
and resolve against the installed headers, so they are not staged.

Two of the three sources are renamed on the way in:

    kern/kern_synch.c    -> kern/pthread_synch.c
    kern/kern_support.c  -> kern/pthread_support.c

kern_synch.c would collide with xnu's own bsd/kern/kern_synch.c, because the
conf system names objects after the basename alone. The new names are not
arbitrary: they are the ones Apple used while this code still lived in xnu, and
bsd/conf/Makefile.template in this very tree still carries

    pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD   += -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
    pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD += -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes

for two files that no longer exist. Renaming to match brings those suppressions
back to the code they were written for; pthread_kext.c gets the same pair.

The three objects are also compiled with -UXNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE and
-UBSD_KERNEL_PRIVATE, which is the other half of putting kext code inside xnu.
These sources are written against the KPI view of the headers -- the view a
kext gets -- and the bsd component defines both of those, which changes what
<sys/user.h> does in two ways that both break the build:

  - XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE makes it include resourcevar.h and signalvar.h, whose
    declarations use proc_t and thread_t, and neither is defined this early in
    the include chain the kext arrives through;

  - BSD_KERNEL_PRIVATE makes it include <sys/pthread_internal.h>, which shares
    its include guard with libpthread's kern_internal.h. That sharing is
    deliberate -- whichever arrives first wins -- but it means that when the
    kext's copy came first, xnu's is skipped, struct ksyn_waitq_element stays
    a forward declaration, and struct uthread embeds an incomplete type.

Undefining both restores exactly the environment the code was written for.
MakeInc.rule puts ${$@_CFLAGS_ADD} after ${CFLAGS} on the command line
(C_RULE_1A), so the -U wins over the -D.

Undefining XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE flips one more line, in osfmk/kern/debug.h:

    #ifndef XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE
    #include <TargetConditionals.h>
    #endif

That include exists for kexts, which are compiled against the SDK and have
the header; a kernel build has no userland headers on its include path, and
putting them there to satisfy one include would cost far more than it buys.
debug.h does not read a single TARGET_OS macro -- the include is purely for
whoever includes debug.h -- so this patch adds a small stand-in beside the
staged sources, reachable through the -I they already have, and defines the
macros from the kernel's own CONFIG_EMBEDDED rather than leaving them absent.

TargetConditionals.h is the only header that has to be supplied this way.
<Availability.h>, which libpthread's qos.h also wants, is already reachable
in this build -- mach/message.h, mach/mach_time.h and libkern/os/log.h all
compile in these same translation units, and all three need its macros. The
libpthread-owned headers qos.h needs (sys/qos.h, sys/qos_private.h and the
sys/_pthread typedefs) are Apple's and are staged by the build script rather
than carried here.

One xnu header has to be corrected rather than worked around. osfmk/vm/pmap.h
declares

    #if CONFIG_PGTRACE
    kern_return_t pmap_pgtrace_fault(pmap_t, vm_map_offset_t, arm_saved_state_t *);
    #endif

outside any privacy guard, while arm_saved_state_t is typedef'd inside
#ifdef XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE in osfmk/mach/arm/thread_status.h. The declaration
is therefore unreadable without XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE -- its parameter type does
not exist -- which has never mattered because everything inside xnu has that
define. The staged sources are the first translation units here that do not,
so the declaration is given the guard its own types already have. Nothing
that has XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE sees any change.

Two more things follow from compiling with the KPI view.

xnu's bsd/pthread/priority_private.h and bsd/pthread/workqueue_syscalls.h
hide everything behind __PTHREAD_EXPOSE_INTERNALS__:

    #if XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE && !defined(__PTHREAD_EXPOSE_INTERNALS__)
    #define __PTHREAD_EXPOSE_INTERNALS__ 1
    #endif
    #ifdef __PTHREAD_EXPOSE_INTERNALS__
    ... everything ...
    #endif

Undefining XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE takes that self-definition with it and both
headers arrive empty. So it is asked for by name instead, which is exactly
what libpthread's own kext build does.

And the arm64 register setup is missing from the open source release. In
_bsdthread_create() and _setup_wqthread(), every published libpthread --
301.50.1, 330.201.1, 330.230.1, 330.250.2 -- has an i386/x86_64 block and
then

    #else
    #error bsdthread_create  not defined for this architecture
    #endif

with no arm branch at all, although the arm stack-address hint further up the
same file is present. The branches are written here from the x86-64 block,
which names its argument order explicitly: those are the System V argument
registers in order, so the AAPCS64 equivalents are x0 through x5, and the
entry point goes in pc with the stack top in sp. AAPCS64 has no red zone, so
sp is the stack top unmodified where x86-64 subtracts C_64_REDZONE_LEN.

This is the one part of this patch that is reconstructed rather than
transplanted, and it cannot be exercised until a userland process calls
bsdthread_create. A 32-bit process would need arguments five and six spilled
onto its stack under AAPCS32; rather than guess that, _bsdthread_create
returns ENOTSUP and _setup_wqthread panics. An arm64 iOS 12 userland has no
32-bit processes, so neither path should ever be taken.

Last, two symbols the kext calls do not exist on arm64. osfmk/vm/vm_user.c
defines the plain mach_vm_map() and mach_vm_allocate() only under

    #if defined(__x86_64__)

above which the comment reads: "These symbols are looked up at runtime by
vmware, VirtualBox, despite not being exported in the symbol sets." The real
entry points everywhere else are mach_vm_map_external() and
mach_vm_allocate_external(), which take the VM tag out of the flags -- which
is exactly how workq_create_threadstack() already passes it, so they are
drop-in. Their prototypes are behind XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE, so they are declared
next to the two defines that redirect the calls, for everything that is not
x86_64.

Linking the kext in rather than loading it also makes two of its globals
collide with xnu's. kern_init.c defines

    pthread_callbacks_t pthread_kern;

and a current_uthread(), because a kext cannot link against the kernel's. Both
already exist here: bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c defines pthread_kern and
initialises it to &pthread_callbacks, and bsd/kern/kern_proc.c defines
current_uthread() doing the same thing. The kext's copies are removed, leaving
the declarations in kern_internal.h to find xnu's. pthread_kext_register()
still takes the address of pthread_kern and sets it to the value it already
holds.

This patch needs pthread-guards-without-kext.patch, which applies before it and
leaves pthread_init() able to survive a NULL table. The two are deliberately
separate and independently removable: the guards are what keeps the boot alive
if the registration ever does not happen, and if this integration has to be
backed out the guards stay behind on their own.

How to tell whether it worked: with the kext registered, pthread_init() prints
nothing. The line

    pthread: kernel extension not loaded; pthread and workqueue syscalls will
    return ENOTSUP

appearing at boot means the table is still NULL and only the guards are holding
the kernel up.

This cannot be tested beyond that until there is a root filesystem. Nothing in
the kernel calls into the table on its own; the first real exercise of it is a
userland process registering its thread start routines.

--- a/bsd/conf/files
+++ b/bsd/conf/files
@@ -385,6 +385,9 @@
 bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c		standard
 bsd/pthread/pthread_priority.c		standard
 bsd/pthread/pthread_workqueue.c		standard
+bsd/pthread/kext/kern/pthread_kext.c	standard
+bsd/pthread/kext/kern/pthread_support.c	standard
+bsd/pthread/kext/kern/pthread_synch.c	standard
 
 bsd/kern/bsd_init.c			standard
 ./init_sysent.c				standard
--- a/bsd/conf/Makefile.template
+++ b/bsd/conf/Makefile.template
@@ -83,6 +83,36 @@
 pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
 pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
 
+# The pthread kext sources staged under bsd/pthread/kext keep libpthread's
+# own layout, because they include each other by project-relative path
+# ("kern/synch_internal.h", "pthread/qos.h", "private/qos_private.h").
+pthread_kext.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -I$(SRCROOT)/bsd/pthread/kext
+pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -I$(SRCROOT)/bsd/pthread/kext
+pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -I$(SRCROOT)/bsd/pthread/kext
+pthread_kext.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
+
+# They are also written against the KPI view of the headers, which is what a
+# kext gets. Compiled inside the bsd component they would get xnu's internal
+# view instead, and <sys/user.h> reacts to both of these: XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE
+# makes it pull in resourcevar.h and signalvar.h, which use proc_t and
+# thread_t before this include chain defines them, and BSD_KERNEL_PRIVATE
+# makes it pull in <sys/pthread_internal.h> -- which shares its include guard
+# with libpthread's kern_internal.h, so the file is skipped and struct uthread
+# ends up embedding an incomplete ksyn_waitq_element. CFLAGS_ADD lands after
+# CFLAGS on the command line (see C_RULE_1A), so these take effect.
+pthread_kext.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -UXNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE -UBSD_KERNEL_PRIVATE
+pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -UXNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE -UBSD_KERNEL_PRIVATE
+pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -UXNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE -UBSD_KERNEL_PRIVATE
+
+# xnu's own pthread headers hide their contents behind
+# __PTHREAD_EXPOSE_INTERNALS__, which they define for themselves when
+# XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE is set. Undefining that above takes the definitions with
+# it, so it is asked for by name -- which is what libpthread's own kext build
+# does. Without it, priority_private.h and workqueue_syscalls.h arrive empty.
+pthread_kext.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -D__PTHREAD_EXPOSE_INTERNALS__=1
+pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -D__PTHREAD_EXPOSE_INTERNALS__=1
+pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -D__PTHREAD_EXPOSE_INTERNALS__=1
+
 # Objects that don't want -Wsign-compare
 OBJS_NO_SIGN_COMPARE =		\
 		radix.o	\
